From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shayne Legault <8741557+shaynelegault@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 12:39:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] mtd: apple-sftl: prevent recursive reclaim during request I/O

Map splits, summary preparation and H2FMI program buffers allocate while
io_lock serializes requests to the logical root disk. GFP_KERNEL reclaim
can otherwise queue writeback against that same disk and wait for the
lock held by the original request.

Use a NOIO allocation scope around all request processing and lower
provider calls, restoring the previous scope on the common completion
path. This keeps existing allocation failure handling while preventing
recursive filesystem I/O from reclaim.

The exact queue-function harness checks balanced scope and completion
on 22 success, policy, deadline and error cases in each write config.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl.rst | 4 +++-
 drivers/mtd/apple-sftl-block.c              | 5 +++++
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl.rst b/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl.rst
index 9d65b2149..6f7a3b229 100644
--- a/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl.rst
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl.rst
@@ -43,7 +43,9 @@ an error. A failed runtime read stops subsequent I/O from this map instance.
 H2FMI unregisters the child before releasing its controller state. The block
 frontend freezes and drains requests during removal. Its request buffer is
 allocated before the disk becomes visible, while blocking requests serialize
-access to that buffer and the provider.
+access to that buffer and the provider. Request processing uses a NOIO
+allocation scope so map and provider buffers cannot trigger recursive
+writeback while the queue lock is held.
 
 The reader has finite operating limits. ``max_page_reads`` defaults to 32,768
 physical page requests and ``max_io_time_ms`` defaults to ten minutes from
diff --git a/drivers/mtd/apple-sftl-block.c b/drivers/mtd/apple-sftl-block.c
index 29a05ae3b..4471a9176 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/apple-sftl-block.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/apple-sftl-block.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/sched/mm.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 
 #include "apple-sftl.h"
@@ -182,10 +183,13 @@ static blk_status_t sftl_queue_rq(struct blk_mq_hw_ctx *hctx,
 	struct request *request = data->rq;
 	struct sftl_disk *sftl = request->q->queuedata;
 	sector_t capacity = get_capacity(sftl->disk);
+	unsigned int noio_flags;
 	blk_status_t status;
 	int ret = 0;
 
 	mutex_lock(&sftl->io_lock);
+	/* Map and provider buffers may allocate while the root disk is busy. */
+	noio_flags = memalloc_noio_save();
 	blk_mq_start_request(request);
 	if (sftl->dead) {
 		ret = -ENODEV;
@@ -203,6 +207,7 @@ static blk_status_t sftl_queue_rq(struct blk_mq_hw_ctx *hctx,
 	} else {
 		ret = sftl_read_request(sftl, request);
 	}
+	memalloc_noio_restore(noio_flags);
 	mutex_unlock(&sftl->io_lock);
 	status = errno_to_blk_status(ret);
 	blk_mq_end_request(request, status);
